// gunbc.host.network_install — one run, one unit, one disk, one install; then it withdraws itself.
//
// PXE 2c. The workflow the plan calls managed_host_network_install: stage the site PXE edge's install binding
// for ONE client, boot that client ONCE over the network, let the installer erase and write ONE pinned disk,
// read the install back, and REMOVE the binding — all in one run, on srv4. Everything that makes this safe to
// hand to a machine is decided here, in pure functions, before any effect: the mode's wet entry reads the
// clock, calls this, and acts only on what this returns.
//
// ONE INSTANT. `decided_at` is the run's own reading of gunbc.clock_read, taken once. It authorizes the disk
// (gunbc.provisioning.install_target_disk admit_install_target_disk compares the reading's instant against
// it) and it is printed in the receipt. There is no decided_at DATA row and there must never be one: review
// 93312 removed exactly that, because a frozen instant makes the staleness check pass forever
// (gunbc.design_argument::real_time_authority).
//
// ONE AUTHORIZATION. The only thing that permits an erase is an Admitted InstallTargetDisk, minted by
// admit_install_target_disk over the PRODUCTION fleet row, this run's instant, and the request's window. The
// same carrier that authorized the erase is the one whose pin the seed renders, so the disk that was
// authorized and the disk the installer is told to wipe are one object rather than two readings that happen
// to agree. gunbc.design_argument::install_authorization_pinpoints_one_target names the root.
//
// WHAT ELSE THIS DECIDES, and in this order, because the order is the safety property:
//   0. THE COMPLETION JOURNAL FIRST, before any BMC write: a (unit, disk serial) already recorded complete
//      refuses the dispatch with InstallAlreadyCompleted. The journal is durable compare-and-set state under
//      gunbc.host_state_root host_state_tree on srv4, read as the run's first effect, so a second dispatch
//      after a completed install refuses before it can touch anything.
//   1. THE HOLD. A durable exclusive hold on the unit for the whole run (std.durable_exclusive_hold, the
//      interlock the arrival door uses), so two concurrent dispatches cannot both read an empty journal and
//      both boot the same machine.
//   2. THE SUBJECT. gunbc.host.install_boot_subject admit_install_boot_subject — an admitted arrival subject
//      with a bound, secured controller whose committed write controller admits the boot-device write. srv15
//      is NOT a managed host and this route does not pretend otherwise.
//   3. THE DISK. admit_install_target_disk, then its pin, then the CONTENTS PRECONDITION below.
//   4. THE BINDING. The edge's install file for this client must be staged AND read back before any boot is
//      attempted at all — a client with no binding gets no controller write, because there is nothing for it
//      to boot into. The staged-and-read-back binding is the gate the dispatch reads.
//   5. THE BOOT. ONE live route: gunbc.machine_intake.oob_boot_handoff, reached through
//      gunbc.host.install_boot_subject install_boot_drive_handoff, which sends the boot-device override ONCE
//      (pxe, EFI — pinned there, not by the caller), reads boot parameter 5 back, and issues the power action
//      ONLY when its own verdict on THAT reading says the override is valid, one-shot, EFI and named. That
//      verdict is the SOLE gate on the destructive step; 2c adds no second judgment over the same bytes, and
//      the install continuation is withheld on every arm short of an issued control.
//   6. THE READ-BACK, then THE WITHDRAWAL, then THE RECORD. Completion is recorded only after the install is
//      read back AND the binding is verified gone; if the binding survives, the run fails and NO completion is
//      written, so a retry is possible instead of a host left with a live installer binding and an
//      "already installed" journal entry.
//
// WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. It does not authorize itself (the per-write approval and the
// approval-broker grant are outside the repo, and select_bmc_write_controller is the model's say-so); it does
// not enroll the unit (PXE 4); and it does not read a credential from argv — the BMC credential travels by the
// stdin/0600 route gunbc.auth.bmc_credential_source already models, which refuses to echo it.
module gunbc.host.network_install

